  Euclid proved around 300 BCE that there are infinitely many primes. His
  argument is short: take any finite list of primes, multiply them together,
  and add 1. The result has no factor in the list, since dividing it by any
  listed prime leaves remainder 1. So it has a prime factor that is not on
  the list. No finite list can contain all primes.
 
  Finding primes is harder than proving they exist. The Sieve of Eratosthenes
  is the classic method. Write down the integers from 2 to N, then
  repeatedly cross out the multiples of each prime you find. Every number
  left standing is prime. The sieve runs in roughly O(N log log N) time,
  which is fast enough for tables of millions of entries. For a single large
  number, trial division by primes up to its square root works, but it
  becomes slow for numbers with hundreds of digits.

  Primality testing is a different problem from factoring. Modern tests such
  as Miller-Rabin are fast and probabilistic: they can declare a number
  composite with certainty, but declare it prime only with very high
  probability. The AKS test, published in 2002, was the first deterministic
  polynomial-time test, though it is too slow for everyday use. Miller-Rabin
  is what most software actually runs.

  Primes matter in practice because factoring large numbers is hard.
  Public-key systems such as RSA rely on the fact that multiplying two large
  primes is easy while recovering them from the product is, as far as anyone
  knows, computationally infeasible at sufficient size. A 2048-bit RSA
  modulus is the product of two roughly 1024-bit primes. Elliptic-curve
  cryptography and Diffie-Hellman rely on related hardness assumptions over
  prime fields. The security of much of the internet's encryption depends on
  the difficulty of this one problem.

  Many questions about primes remain open. The Twin Prime Conjecture asserts
  there are infinitely many pairs of primes differing by 2, such as (11, 13)
  and (17, 19). In 2013, Yitang Zhang proved that infinitely many pairs
  differ by some fixed bound below 70 million, and subsequent work reduced
  that bound to 246. The conjecture itself is still unproven. Goldbach's
  Conjecture, that every even integer greater than 2 is a sum of two primes,
  has been checked by computer far beyond 10^18 but has no proof.

  The Prime Number Theorem describes how primes thin out. The count of primes
  up to x is approximately x / ln(x). The Riemann Hypothesis, one of the
  most famous unsolved problems in mathematics, concerns the error term in
  this approximation and is equivalent to a precise statement about where the
  zeros of the Riemann zeta function lie. A proof would give far tighter
  control over the distribution of primes than anything currently known.

  Primes are simple to define and difficult to fully understand. They appear
  in number theory, cryptography, and computing, and their deepest patterns
  remain unresolved.
 
